  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Let $u$ and $v$ be vectors such that $\u2016u\u2016=6\\sqrt{2}$ and $\u2016u+2v\u2016=6\\sqrt{6}.$  If $u$ is orthogonal to $v$, find $\u2016v\u2016.$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $u$ is orthogonal to $v,$ we also have that $u$ is orthogonal to $2v.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2016u+2v{\u2016}^{2} & =\u2016u{\u2016}^{2}+\u20162v{\u2016}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2016u+2v{\u2016}^{2} & =\u2016u\u2016+(\u20162v\u2016)^{2} \\\\ \u2016u+2v{\u2016}^{2} & =\u2016u{\u2016}^{2}+4\u2016v{\u2016}^{2} \\\\ (6\\sqrt{6})^{2} & =(6\\sqrt{2})^{2}+4\u2016v{\u2016}^{2} \\\\ 216 & =72+4\u2016v{\u2016}^{2} \\\\ 144 & =4\u2016v{\u2016}^{2} \\\\ \u2016v{\u2016}^{2} & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\u2016v\u2016=6.$",
